<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">L'Orfeo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1607">1607</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">L'Orfeo is an opera by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudio-monteverdi">Claudio Monteverdi</a> to a libretto by Alessandro Striggio, first performed at the Gonzaga court in Mantua in 1607. Retelling the myth of Orpheus and Eurydice, it is one of the earliest operas and the oldest still regularly performed. Its large orchestra and expressive writing set a model for the new genre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/l-orfeo.jpg" alt="L'Orfeo" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cesare Gennari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesare_Gennari_Orfeo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudio-monteverdi">Claudio Monteverdi</a> was born in Cremona in 1567 and joined the court of Duke Vincenzo Gonzaga at Mantua as a viola player in 1590 or 1591. He rose to become master of music in 1601. The librettist, Alessandro Striggio, was a young lawyer and diplomat at the Gonzaga court and a member of the Accademia degli Invaghiti, Mantua's intellectual society, which was the chief outlet for its theatrical works.</p><p>Work on the libretto was under way in January 1607, when Francesco Gonzaga wrote asking his brother for a high-quality castrato for a 'play in music' being prepared for Carnival. By then Monteverdi had sixteen years of experience of stage music. The premiere was on 24 February 1607, a date established by two letters of 23 February, probably in a room of the apartments of the Duke's widowed sister, Margherita Gonzaga d'Este.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The action alternates between the fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and the Underworld. La musica sings a prologue and introduces Orfeo, who celebrates his wedding to Euridice. A messenger brings news that Euridice has died of a snakebite while gathering flowers, and Orfeo resolves to bring her back. Speranza guides him to the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>, where the ferryman Caronte refuses him passage until Orfeo plays his lyre and lulls him to sleep.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Proserpina</a> persuades her husband Plutone to release Euridice on condition that Orfeo not look back as he leads her out. Distracted by a noise offstage, he looks round and she vanishes. In the printed score <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> then descends and takes Orfeo to the heavens, where he will see Euridice's likeness in the stars. Striggio's 1607 libretto instead had Orfeo threatened by a chorus of Bacchantes, and his fate left uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Monteverdi listed around 41 instruments in his score, using distinct groups to depict scenes: strings, harpsichords and recorders for the fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and heavy brass for the underworld. He published the score in 1609 and again in 1615, and after his death in 1643 the opera went unperformed for many years.</p><p>Robert Eitner published an abridged score in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1881, and Vincent d'Indy's edition formed the basis of a concert performance at the Schola Cantorum on 25 February 1904 and of the first modern staged performance at the Théâtre Réjane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, on 2 May 1911. The first recording was issued in 1939, and the 1969 recording by Nikolaus Harnoncourt and the Vienna Concentus Musicus was praised for making the music sound as Monteverdi imagined. The quatercentenary in 2007 was marked by performances around the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “L'Orfeo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
